/**
 * Lesson 531 · C2 · Puntuación avanzada: punto y coma, dos puntos e incisos (course unit G231).
 * Orthography, strictly per the RAE-ASALE Ortografía de la lengua española (2010). Builds on G197
 * (g497: incisos between commas, vocatives, punctuation of connectors, rayas and paréntesis, no
 * comma between subject and verb) and G203 (g503: commas in relative clauses); those cores are
 * not repeated. Content: semicolon between closely related independent clauses, in complex
 * enumerations whose items carry commas (comma before the final «y», as in the Ortografía's
 * examples), before sin embargo / no obstante / por tanto when the clauses are long (very long →
 * full stop), and optionally before pero when the members are long and contain commas; lowercase
 * after it; comma, never semicolon, after a fronted subordinate clause. Colon: announcing an
 * enumeration after an anticipating element (tres, los siguientes, estos), closing an enumeration
 * before the word that sums it up, cause / consequence / conclusion / explanation between clauses
 * without a link, quotations in direct speech, letter greetings (Estimada Sra. López:); lowercase
 * after it in running text except quotations and letters; no colon between a verb or preposition
 * and its complement (*Los ingredientes son: …), none before indirect speech (*Dijo: que…), and
 * the style advice to avoid two colons in one sentence. Nested incisos: paréntesis inside rayas,
 * rayas inside paréntesis, corchetes inside paréntesis; corchetes for interpolations and […] in
 * quotations. Quotation marks: « » then “ ” then ‘ ’; the sentence's full stop always after the
 * closing quote, also when the quote ends with ? or !. Ellipsis: three dots, never four, never
 * after etc.; may be followed by a comma. ¿? ¡! opened where the question starts; lowercase after
 * them when the sentence continues (¿Quién?, ¿cuándo?). Ampliación: CERTIFICA: Que…, corchetes
 * for interpolations, ellipsis + comma, series of questions.
 * Scene: Universidad de Montealto. Lucía Arrieta (professor of History, editor of the collective
 * volume «Agua y ciudad», on the city's network of irrigation channels, 16th–19th c.) revises the
 * eleven chapters; Gabriel Ortega, postdoctoral researcher, writes the central chapter; Marisa
 * López directs the university press. The chronicler Albornoz is fictional. Escuchar: Lucía's
 * voice message to the authors (es-MX-DaliaNeural, ustedes). Escribir: Lucía's email to Sra.
 * López. Lucía and Gabriel use tú. Instructions use tú.
 */
